eded. But since T4 is a terminal? No, T4 is simple (2-8 are simple).  \n\nAlso check possible patterns: I have B6 pung (dragon? no, it's simple), T7-9 chow (upper tiles). If I keep B7 and B8 pairs, I could aim for Upper Tiles (tiles 7-9 only) since my hand has T7,T8,T9 chow, B7 pair, B8 pair, plus maybe draw B9. But I have T2,T4 which are not 7-9, so must discard them. Upper Tiles is 24 points if complete, but I also have B6 pung which is 6, so not pure upper tiles unless I change B6 later (unlikely).  \n\nBetter to aim for All Simples (no terminals/honors) since my hand has no terminals/honors now (T2,T4,B5,B7,B8 are all simples; B6 pung is simple; T7-9 chow has terminals? T9 is terminal, so T9 in chow breaks All Simples. So All Simples not possible.  \n\nLikely just go for efficient shape: keep pairs and hope to draw pungs or chows.  \n\nDiscard T2 because it has fewer remaining copies and no good connections.  \n\nReason:  \nT2 is a singleton with very few remaining tiles (only 1 left) and poor chow potential since T1 is exhausted (0 left) and T3 is scarce (1 left). Keeping the B5, B7, B8 pairs offers more flexibility to form pungs or chows, and these tiles have more copies available. Discarding T2 now reduces the hand\u2019s deadwood while preserving options.  \n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
